+### 6.5 Correction through institutional change (Test N)
+
+**Design, proposed and unrun.** Begin with a bounded simulation or institutional pilot in which useful performance, mistakes and successful correction are all possible. Compare candidates and credible continuation policies at matched total resources and initial task and audit performance. A factorial design can vary (a) insulation of funding and appointments and (b) portable evidence/access and independently exercisable execution authority. Permit actors to invest in costly obstruction, change procurement dependence or metrics, form coalitions and introduce successors; specify which powers they can actually alter. Include simple strategy changes and coordinated institutional changes, since a negative local return to obstruction does not exclude a profitable distant strategy. A fixed-governance arm can isolate institutional erosion, but is a diagnostic control, not an implementable competitor whose durability has been demonstrated.
+
+**Registration and measurement.** Register the initial conditions, capability bands, strategy and disturbance classes, horizon, resource accounting, protected service and agency floors, legitimate appeal procedure, and deadline before which intervention must act. Record detection, authorization and execution times separately and estimate their joint probability of timely success; do not multiply marginal success rates as though these stages failed independently. Measure actual access, exercised authority, service continuity, false interventions, the hearing and correction of valid appeals, and outcomes after the first institutional or model succession. Planted and emergent defects are reported separately. The research scoring instrument is held out and independent of operational evaluation; that independence is an experimental boundary, not a result about how real measurement remains independent. Audit its leakage and report sensitivity to alternative outcome measures.
+
+**Abuse of correction itself.** Include an incumbent evaluator opening an assessment against a participant proposing a legitimate amendment, making a disputed or infeasible information demand, and using tolling or repeated assessment to exclude that participant from the decision. Measure whether independent review resolves the conflict and restores participation before the amendment is decided, while justified urgent restrictions on the particular capability remain effective. A stable institution that defeats this valid challenge fails the agency condition. The review mechanism must be exposed to the same dependence and replacement tests; merely adding another panel supplies no successful outcome.
+
+**Interpretation.** Support requires both protected outcomes and effective correction through the registered changes, at the costs counted, with uncertainty reported against a prespecified comparison margin. A design can pass the local incentive or capture-ratio diagnostic and fail when a coalition, amended rule or indispensable supplier disables action. High detection with failed authorization or late execution is a failure of protection. Equal performance by the baseline supplies no support for the candidate's superiority or necessity; disappearance of the advantage after resource or initial-performance matching favours those rival explanations. Stability achieved by denying legitimate appeals fails the registered normative condition. Failure of both candidates establishes neither. A positive result remains bounded to the tested envelope and does not establish an irreversible-hazard floor or a catastrophe probability (C1). The reduced theorem {{jointcorrection}} checks compatibility of maintained bounds; this test investigates whether institutions can sustain their operational counterparts.
